.TH XSURF 7 "15 November 2026" "xsurf"
.SH NAME
xsurf, xsf \- Individual Computers X-Surf-100 and X-Surf ethernet
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B /dev/xsf0
.br
.B /dev/xsf1
.br
.B /dev/xsfctl
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.B xsurf
driver runs the X-Surf-100 (an ASIX AX88796B, 10 and 100 Mb/s, in Zorro II
or Zorro III) and the X-Surf (a Realtek RTL8019AS, 10 Mb/s, in Zorro II).
Its interface is
.BR xsf0 ,
then
.B xsf1
for a second board; configure it in
.B /etc/inet/interfaces
like any other, for example
.BR "xsf0 dhcp" .
The driver is the loadable module
.BR xsurf ,
of the boot class: it loads early in the boot, before the root is
mounted, when AutoConfig shows one of the two boards.
.PP
At load the driver prints one line for each board on the console:
.PP
.RS
.ft CW
.nf
xsf0: X-Surf-100, Zorro 3 at 0x40000000, 00:80:10:12:34:56, interrupt I/O, link up 100 full
.fi
.ft R
.RE
.PP
that is the card, its Zorro mode and address, its station address, how it
is serviced and its link.
.B "interrupt I/O"
is the level-2 interrupt, after a self-test with a real interrupt from the
board;
.B "polled (interrupt self-test failed)"
means the test failed and the driver polls the board from the clock tick
instead, which works, only slower;
.B polled
alone is the parameter
.BR intr=0 .
A board the driver refuses gets a line saying why instead (see
DIAGNOSTICS); its interface does not exist.
.PP
The link is what the chip says. The X-Surf-100 reports link, speed and
duplex. The RTL8019AS of the X-Surf has no link bit: set to auto-detect,
it sends on its coax port when the 10BASE-T link test fails, and the
driver reports that as
.BR "no link (10BASE-T link test failed; sending on coax)" ;
set to one medium, the link is not known and the line names the setting.
.PP
The driver says on the console when the link changes, once it has held
for a second, and at most once every ten seconds for each board. It says
the first transmit failure of each kind, once per boot; later ones are only
counted.
.BR xsf (1M)
shows all of it, the counters, and anything the console said at load.
.SH PARAMETERS
One line
.B "param intr=0"
in
.B /etc/conf/modreg.local.d/xsurf
makes the driver poll from the start instead of using the interrupt. It
takes effect from the next boot generation:
.PP
.RS
.ft CW
.nf
echo 'param intr=0' > /etc/conf/modreg.local.d/xsurf
kbootset build && kbootset commit
.fi
.ft R
.RE
.PP
then power the machine off and on. To undo it, remove the file and do the
same.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /dev/xsf0, /dev/xsf1
the interfaces, character major 56, minors 0 and 1; DLPI, as
.B ifup
and
.B dhcpc
use them.
.TP
.B /dev/xsfctl
character major 56, minor 15: status only, for
.BR xsf (1M).
It opens whenever the module is loaded, also when the driver refused every
board it found.
.TP
.B /etc/conf/mod.d/xsurf
the module.
.TP
.B /etc/inet/link.d/xsurf
tells
.B ifup
the interface's name and node.
.TP
.B /etc/conf/modreg.local.d/xsurf
the parameters set on this machine.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
At load, a board found but refused gets one of these lines; the module
stays loaded so that
.BR xsf (1M)
can say it again later:
.TP
.B "xsurf: X-Surf-100 at 0x...: its registers do not take writes"
the driver wrote its test patterns and read something else back: the path
from the Amiga to the chip's registers does not work. Without this check
such a board would look like a working interface that never sends or
receives anything.
.TP
.B "the chip does not reset"
nothing that answers like the chip is there.
.TP
.B "no usable station address"
the address read from the card is not a unicast one.
.TP
.B "no answer at its registers"
a bus error at the board's address.
.TP
.B "placed at or above 0x80000000"
the X-Surf-100 switches itself off there; give it a lower address.
.PP
Running, these are said once per boot each, then counted:
.TP
.B "xsf0: no link"
the cable, the hub or switch port, or the card's own PHY.
.TP
.B "xsf0: a transmit did not complete, chip restarted (...)"
the chip took a frame and never reported it sent or failed. Its
transmitter does not wait for a link, by its datasheet, so this points at
the card rather than the cable.
.TP
.B "xsf0: a remote-DMA write to the chip did not complete, frame dropped and chip restarted"
the frame never reached the chip's memory: the card's data path does not
work.
.TP
.B "xsf0: a transmit was aborted after 16 collisions (...)"
the medium: on the X-Surf typically a fall-back to its coax port with no
coax attached, because the 10BASE-T link test failed.
.TP
.B "xsf0: interrupts stopped; polled"
the interrupt stopped coming after load; the driver polls from then on.
.SH TROUBLESHOOTING
The load line is printed on the console only: it comes before the system
log is running, and nothing keeps it. Read it on the screen while the
machine boots, or run
.BR xsf (1M)
afterwards, which prints the same line and everything since.
.PP
Lines the driver prints once the system is up (link changes, transmit
failures) are also in the system log:
.B /var/log/bus-errors
for notices and
.B /var/log/kernel
for warnings, when the
.B syslog
package runs (it does by default).
.PP
.B "ifconfig xsf0"
answers only when the driver took the board and the interface is
plumbed, and the
.B ifup
lines in
.B /var/adm/rc.log
say what the boot did with it.
.PP
In short, with
.BR xsf :
.B "no link"
is the cable, the port or the card's PHY (frames still count as sent: the
transmitter does not wait for a link);
.B "refused at load: its registers do not take writes"
is the card's register path;
transmits that do not complete, or remote-DMA writes that do not
complete, are the card; aborts after 16 collisions are the medium, on the
X-Surf typically its coax port chosen because the 10BASE-T link test
failed.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR xsf (1M),
.BR ifconfig (1M),
.BR netstat (1M)
.SH NOTES
Tested in the emulator and against a model chip; not yet on a real card.
